# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# Snapshot mode (pf_ring with bounded = false) deliberately lets producers
# overwrite records a reader may be inspecting: keeping the most recent
# window is the entire point, so there is no backpressure to stop them.
# The reader guards against it with a seqlock rather than a lock -- the lap
# check in pf_ring_next opens it, pf_ring_still_valid closes it, and a
# record that was rewritten in between is discarded instead of trusted.
#
# TSan cannot see that contract. It reports the producer's plain stores
# into a record body against the reader's load of the same bytes, which is
# exactly the interleaving the seqlock exists to tolerate. The races it
# flags are confined to the ring's data area and never involve head, tail
# or a record tag -- those are all atomic and genuinely race-free, which is
# what the rest of the test suite checks.
#
# Bounded (continuous) mode has no such race at all: producers refuse to
# claim space the reader has not released, so a published record is stable
# until the reader commits past it.
#
# emit_event is the library's producer filling a record body; drain_locked
# is the reader copying one out before encoding (the copy is precisely what
# makes a concurrent overwrite harmless). fill_rec/check_rec are the unit
# test's equivalents.
race:^emit_event$
race:^drain_locked$
race:^fill_rec$
race:^check_rec$
